{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e4d4f7c\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nCompress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all\nforeground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.\n\nRule\nKeep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.\nOutput row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are\nrecolored pink.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated\nrow is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e734a0e8\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nCopy the unique colored sprite tile into every tiled cell marked by an internal separator-colored pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- Full same-color separator rows and columns partition the grid into equal rectangular tiles.\n- One tile contains the only non-background, non-separator colored sprite.\n- Target tiles are otherwise background-colored but contain at least one internal pixel of the separator color.\n\nRule\n- Keep the separator lines and the source sprite tile unchanged.\n- Replace each marked target tile with an exact copy of the source tile.\n- Unmarked background-only tiles remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The internal separator-colored pixels are placement markers; the single colored tile is the template to stamp into those marked cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"35ab12c3\" lines=\"73-88\">\n<description>\nComplete outlined shapes and carry attached shadow pixels along their edges.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Each active color marks vertices of an 8-direction aligned outline.\n- A start vertex can have adjacent non-bg, different-colored shadow pixels.\n- Other colored pixels may be distractors unless they lie on valid drawable edges.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, order its pixels by polar angle around their centroid to form a cycle.\n- Draw only horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree edges whose interiors are clear.\n- From the chosen start vertex, copy adjacent shadow offsets along each drawable edge\n  when the parallel path is in bounds and clear.\n\nKey insight: The main outline is reconstructed first, then the start vertex's neighbor offsets define parallel shadow strokes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"522fdd07\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nCycle each solid odd-sided square to the next smaller centered square, wrapping a single pixel to a 9x9 square.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a separated 4-connected monochrome solid square.\n- Square side lengths are odd and belong to the cycle 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- Keep each square's color and center.\n- Replace side length n by n - 2 when n > 1; replace side length 1 by side length 9.\n\nKey insight:\n- The visible operation is on square size, not color: side lengths cycle 1 -> 9 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5623160b\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nPush every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Maroon pixels are fixed markers.\n- Every other foreground 4-connected object touches the marker pixels on exactly one cardinal side.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each non-background, non-maroon object from its original position.\n- Translate it in the direction away from its maroon contact until the object reaches the grid boundary.\n- Keep the maroon marker pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The maroon contact side is a launcher: it determines a unique outward ray for the whole object, and the edge of the grid determines the translation distance.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3cd86f4f\" lines=\"6-18\">\n<description>\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\n\nRule\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\n\nKey insight\nThe colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so\nthe left edge forms a descending diagonal.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d255d7a7\" lines=\"99-114\">\n<description>\nMove the patterned edge band to the opposite edge along every row or column whose band slice contains the trail color.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- The two foreground colors are a frequent trail color and a rarer marker color.\n- All marker pixels lie nearest to one grid edge, and that edge contains trail-color runs that define the source-band thickness.\n\nRule\n- Choose the source edge identified by the marker pixels.\n- A row or column perpendicular to that edge moves if its source-band slice contains the trail color.\n- Copy that slice unchanged to the opposite edge, erase the remaining trail cells to background, and keep marker-only lines fixed.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color identifies the active edge, while the trail color identifies which parallel rows or columns carry an edge-band slice across the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dfadab01\" lines=\"79-93\">\n<description>\nExpand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.\n- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.\n\nRule:\n- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.\n- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5545f144\" lines=\"191-207\">\n<description>\nMove a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.\n- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the rightmost frame.\n- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable\n  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the\n  nearest one.\n- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.\n- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.\n\nKey insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"973e499e\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nExpand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.\n- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.\n- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.\n\nRule\n- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.\n- Inside the block, write c exactly where the original input also has color c.\n- Write black everywhere else in the block.\n\nKey insight\nEach pixel selects the global mask of its own color; the result is the blockwise self-mask of the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"668eec9a\" lines=\"29-47\">\n<description>\nSummarize the foreground line objects as ordered horizontal color stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background fills a rectangular grid.\n- Each non-background color forms one contiguous one-cell-thick line segment.\n- The line segments have distinct topmost rows, and there are at most five of\n  them.\n\nRule:\n- Sort the foreground colors by the topmost row occupied by their line\n  segment.\n- Create a 5x3 output filled with the background color.\n- Place one solid 1x3 row for each foreground color, preserving that\n  top-to-bottom order and padding any unused rows above with background.\n\nKey insight: The exact line length and slope do not matter; only the\nvertical order of the colored line segments is encoded in the output stack.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"60d73be6\" lines=\"22-39\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that\nfills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground\npixels are sparse markers away from that cross.\n\nRule\nKeep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the\npositions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross\ncolumn, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are\nclipped.\n\nKey insight\nThe full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for\ncompleting the quadrant pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4a1cacc2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
